urin type formula that computes the number of lattice points in polytopes. Our formula is obtained by computing a symmetric expression for the Todd class of the permutohedral variety. By showing that this formula does not always have positive values, we prove that the Todd class of the permutohedral variety {X}_{d} is not effective for d\\ge 24.\n\nAdditionally, we prove that the linear coefficient in the Ehrhart polynomial of any lattice generalized permutohedron is positive.\n\nReçu le : 2020-04-20\nRévisé le : 2020-10-07\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFu Liu - Research (https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~fuliu/research.html)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; As a consequence of this result, we conclude that there exists an order polytope that is not Ehrhart positivie for any dimension d ≥ 14, and for any positive integer k, there exists an order polytope whose Ehrhart polynomial has precisely k negative coefficients. ...      Continuing work in Ehrhart positivity for generalized permutohedra, we use Berline-Vergne's valuation to study our conjecture on the Ehrhart positivity of generalized permutohedra, and the stronger conjecture that the Berline-Vergne valuation is positive on regular permutohedra.\n  15. On positivity of Ehrhart polynomials\nRecent Trends in Algebraic Combinatorics (2019), pp 189-237. Association for Women in Mathematics Series, Vol 16. Springer, Cham.\n     Ehrhart polynomial counts the number of lattice points inside dilations of integral polytopes. We say a polytope is Ehrhart positive if all the coefficients of its Ehrhart polynomial are positive, and ask when a polytope is Ehrhart positive. In this article, we discuss techniques attacking this problem, and survey known results (positive and negative) on interesting famiies of polytopes.\n  16. Berline-Vergne valuation and generalized permutohedra, with Federico Castillo\nDiscrete and Computational Geometry 60 (2018), no. 4, 885-908. journal online version\n     Continuing work in Ehrhart positivity for generalized permutohedra, we use Berline-Vergne's valuation to study our conjecture on the Ehrhart positivity of generalized permutohedra, and the stronger conjecture that the Berline-Vergne valuation is positive on regular permutohedra. We show our conjectures hold for dimension up to 6, and for faces of codimension up to 3. We also give two equivalent statements to the second conjecture in terms of mixed valuations and Todd class, respectively.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAntimagic Directed Graphs (https://dwest.web.illinois.edu/regs/amagdig.html)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.9 years ago; Crawled: 13.4 years ago; Conjecture 2: Every simple connected graph has an antimagic orientation. ... Schwartz, On antimagic directed graphs, J Graph Theory 64 (2010), pp.\n# Antimagic Directed Graphs (2009)\n\nOriginator(s): D. Hefetz, T. Mütze, and J. Schwartz    (presented by Sarah Behrens - REGS 2013)\n\nDefinitions: An antimagic labeling of a graph or digraph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges is a bijection $f\\colon\\,E(G)\\rightarrow\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ such that the values at the vertices are distinct, where the value at $v$ in the undirected case is the sum of the labels on edges incident to $v$, and in the directed case it is the sum of the labels on entering edges minus the sum of the labels on exiting edges. A [di]graph having an antimagic labeling is antimagic.\n\nBackground: It was conjectured by Hartsfield and Ringel [HR] that every simple connected graph on at least three vertices is antimagic. Alon et al. [AKLRY] proved this when the minimum degree is sufficiently large. Hefetz, Saluz, and Tran [HST] proved that graphs on $p^k$ vertices (when $p$ is prime) are antimagic when they have a $2$-factor consisting of $p$-cycles. Other progress towards the conjecture can be found in Gallian's survey [G]. Hefetz, Mütze, and Schwartz [HMS] studied the analogous problem for digraphs.\n\nQuestion 1: Is every connected digraph on at least four vertices antimagic?\n\nComment: Both $K_3$ with cyclic orientation and $P_3$ with consistent orientation are not antimagic. [HMS] showed that all orientations of stars, wheels, and complete graphs are antimagic (with at least four vertices), as are dense graphs (large minimum degree). One can consider a weaker version.\n\nConjecture 2: Every simple connected graph has an antimagic orientation.\n\nComment: The families of graphs shown in [HMS] to satisfy Conjecture 2 are larger: ($2d+1$)-regular graphs, $2d$-regular connected graphs having a matching covering all but at most one vertex, $2n$-vertex graphs having a perfect matching and an independent set of size $n$ whose vertices all have degree at least $3$. These classes do not even cover all $2$-regular graphs:\n\nQuestion 3: Does every disjoint union of cycles admit an antimagic orientation?\n\nComment: Given an antimagic labeling of a simple bipartite graph, an antimagic orientation can be contracted by directing all edges from one part to the other. This strategy does not work when odd cycles are present.\n\nReferences:\n[AKLRY] N. Alon, G. Kaplan, A. Lev, Y. Roditty, and R. Yuster, Dense graphs are antimagic, J Graph Theory 47 (2004), pp. 297-309.\n[G] J.A. Gallian, A dynamic survey of graph labeling, Electronic J Combinatorics (1997-2009).\n[HR] N. Hartsfield and G. Ringel, Pearls in Graph Theory, Academic Press, Boston,1990, pp. 108-109 (revised version, 1994).\n[HMS] D. Hefetz, T. Mütze, and J. Schwartz, On antimagic directed graphs, J Graph Theory 64 (2010), pp. 219-232.\n[HST] D. Hefetz, A. Saluz, and H.T.T. Tran, An application of the Combinatorial Nullstellensatz to a graph labeling problem, J Graph Theory 65 (2010), pp. 70-82.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Todd class of the permutohedral variety (https://www.researchgat